comments[5]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-07T08:33:40.505Z","harness:codex","GitHub source: https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/issues/919. Duplicate check searched all 2,351 items and every status for _workspace,history-author-acknowledge,malformed coordinates,author attribution,and GH-919; no canonical issue existed."
  "2026-08-07T14:58:10.087Z","harness:codex","Final evidence: ordinary item IDs and _workspace:<line> coordinates now share one selector parser, conflict policy, and transport helper. CLI duplicate logic was removed; MCP delegates the same SDK contract. All author-attribution, catalog, contract, and exact coverage gates pass."
  "2026-08-07T15:13:00.623Z","harness:codex","PR #929 review found that shared author-acknowledgment argument failures emit usage exit code 2 while generated catalog heuristics still classify selector conflict and target failures as conflict/generic failure. Reopened to make the public implementation, catalog generator, and tests agree."
  "2026-08-07T15:24:32.002Z","harness:codex","Review implementation evidence: the generated public error catalog now explicitly classifies SDK wrapper codes history_author_acknowledge_selector_conflict, target_not_actionable, and target_unreadable as usage exit 2, matching runtime behavior; generator regression coverage added."
  "2026-08-07T15:48:47.471Z","harness:codex","Final verification: linked generator, author-attribution, SDK integrity, and CLI integration tests passed through pm test; contracts and generated error catalog are current and exact runtime usage exits are preserved."
learnings[2]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-07T14:58:10.600Z","harness:codex","Synthetic workspace coordinates are first-class history selectors, not malformed item IDs. Parsing them once in the SDK prevents transport-specific acknowledgment gaps and error-code drift."
  "2026-08-07T15:48:48.073Z","harness:codex","When a shared SDK refusal helper emits a stable transport exit, generated catalogs need explicit overrides for wrapper codes whose names otherwise imply the wrong generic class."
